I do not know. They might find that Ottaw'a has benefited sufficiently by the increase in government activities in the city. I am open-minded on the subject. I do not know; I am not an expert. They might advance good reasons why the amount should be increased. But everything which has been brought to Ottawa, including, as mentioned by the hon. member for Rosetown-Biggar (Mr. Coldwell) the increase of population and the extension of building, might have to be taken into consideration in determining the amount which should be fixed. At the present time I believe that for the services
72537-148}
received we can still negotiate with the city authorities and increase, as we did last year, the amount paid for the use of water. For water we paid $50,000 in 1941, and last year I believe we paid $81,000 on the increased basis. But I do not see that by the appointment of a committee now we would make very much headway.

More than that when water rates are considered. The amount of $333,000 is not all spent within Ottawa;
IMr. Mcllraith.]
some of it is spent in the surrounding district. A total of $80,000 was paid out last year for water taxes.

and incur heavy capital expenditures upon which in the end interest must be paid. But if there are many instances in peace time where that is not justified, surely with a temporary condition such as we have during this war-time period, this government ought to review its position with regard to the whole question of purchasing property for temporary purposes. I do not know what the government is going to do with some of the property it has bought. In many of the instances that have come to the attention of hon. members from time to time I am quite sure there could not have been any real necessity for putting so much capital into real estate holdings which were to be used for temporary, war-time purposes. Under the War Measures Act and the Natural Resources Mobilization Act the government has full power to make any move it may deem desirable or expedient in the public interest. It does not have to expropriate; it does not have to purchase. It may rent; it may lease; it has wide and comprehensive powers which give it those rights and privileges. So that I think it is incumbent upon the Minister of Public Works, when we begin to consider his estimates, to make some declaration or announcement with regard to the policy which I think is running this country into a tremendous sum of money in capital expenditures which, I am afraid, in the years that lie ahead will be against the interests of the taxpayers, and which will make this government and this country look very foolish from a business point of view when hostilities* come to an end.
I do not wish to overemphasize my point;
I do not wish to exaggerate the position, but I think someone in this house must ask for a halt in what I think is almost approaching a landslide with regard to the matter of purchasing buildings for temporary accommodation. There may be some instances in which it is justified, but it seems to me such instances are rather rare in comparison with the many examples of the other kind that are too apparent, I think, for any of us to overlook. Therefore I would ask the minister now to give us a declaration as to the government policy which has dictated, this kind of spends ing and this sort of investment of the funds of the people at a time when those funds are difficult to obtain. I should be glad if the minister would give the committee the benefit of his views in that regard. .
